			<div class="content" id="post-content-4822813">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre:</strong> Urban Legend</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> Standard description, some interview</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> SCP-XXXX is a beaten backpack with UC, Santa Cruz, patches on it. Once opened the person opening it is slowly replaced, physically and mentally, with a young philosophy student who then returns to exploring the continental United States. He also writes about his experiences, which helps pay his way.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> In 1989 Charles Anton Page decided to drop out of college and explore America; he felt trapped and needed to run away. He quickly adopted a &quot;one week at a time&quot; philosophy, where he'd find odd jobs and beg in order to obtain enough to buy a bus ticket and some food, and then would take off on the next leg of his journey.</p>
<p>A year or so later, he was robbed and left for dead, and would eventually succumb to his wounds. The body was never recovered. His backpack, and the contents therein, were pawned at a local pawn shop. Someone bought the backpack, and over the next few hours was transformed into a virtual clone of Page, but without the knowledge of his death or that he was leading an extended life.</p>
<p>He is able to live for one week (168 hours) at a time before copies of the wounds that killed him appear on his body, and he then quickly succumbs to them. Once someone finds and opens the backpack, he is yet again allowed to live for another week, but with the usual memory lapse.</p>
<p>While he has adapted to the modern world somewhat (he writes a blog instead of magazine articles), he is still driven to explore the continental United States. He just does it one week at a time.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention Grabber:</strong> A basic urban legend about a youth cut short but still trying to find a way to accomplish his dream of exploring the United States through a mix of odd jobs, bus tickets, and hitchhiking, one week at a time.</p>

