														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4822908">
									

<p>1) His memory is basically a sliding scale; he remembers most of what he's encountered but he has problems with time (so he could place the events in order but not associate those events with a specific time). He also forgets that he dies; he has no idea that he's an anomaly or that he's only alive a week at a time now. He just has an urgency to get things done as quickly as possible.</p>
<p>2) He's not an agent of any GOI. In fact, it was only someone noting the dates and locations of his blog posts that happened to correlate to some missing people that the Foundation even had a clue he existed. (Given the nature of anomalies, I don't see it as a major stretch that the Foundation tracks when people go missing.) While he could theoretically be a decent operative for a GOI, the limitations on his memories make him more of a liability, and he couldn't be used as a reliable means to eliminate someone as you would need to guarantee that the person would a) pick the pack up, and b) would be the person to encounter the pack.</p>
<p>3) The transformation requires possession of the backpack and takes about three hours. The transformation could theoretically be reversed if the person stopped carrying the backpack after an hour or two; the biological template would then reassert itself. After that, it's too late. When the transformation is complete, he gains some of their skills (but they usually only last through the current incarnation) and the person's biometrics (such as fingerprints) but loses the bulk of their memories.</p>

							</div>

								
					
