<p>You really can't assume anything with anomalies. There are a number of items in the archives that involve this sort of thing, and it does indeed get awkward. That is a good thing for narrative purposes. <a href="/scp-3009">SCP-3009</a> for instance revolves around a situation where the Foundation can't figure out whether one, both, or neither of a pair are the original person. There are anomalies that involve things like ambiguous duplication of a person obliviously living an otherwise normal life elsewhere, and others where it's implied that the anomaly involves an afterimage or reflection of a person and their desires at a certain moment in their lives.</p>
<p>The fact that this is a Philosophy student opens up some really cool opportunities on that front that I'd HIGHLY recommend embracing and running with. The meaning of &quot;self&quot; when we all shed identities over time, the search for truth and meaning, what constitutes a fulfilling life, whether there's any real value in contemplating paths not taken&#8230; These are are all long-standing philosophical topics that get <em>extremely</em> relevant to this anomaly if in 1989 Charles Anton Page changed his major from philosophy to business and is currently employed as a Junior Partner at a minor accounting firm in Oakland.</p>

